=> three schedulings (10 static / 200 pooled / 10 static capped), two budget settings, one catalog.

WORK CONCENTRATION — the reliable finding. shard-seconds total 56,990 over 200 shards:
  s77 16,770   s27 16,707   s125 7,347   s76 2,361   s26 1,912   ...
Two shards hold 33,477 s = 59% of all work; s77 alone is 29%. Depth-1 sharding is EXHAUSTED: no pool
can finish before 16,770 s while one shard carries 29% of the search. Over-decomposition to 200 did
not break the monsters up, because a shard is a residue class and a single first-type subtree is
indivisible under this partition. Depth-2 sharding (split on the first EXTENSION, not just the first
type) is the only route left for parallelism here.

⚑ TIMING COMPARISON IS VOID, do not cite it. Pool 17,515 s vs static 7,857 s reads as 2.2x slower and
cannot be: s77 is a subset of the static run's w7 (77 = 7 mod 10), so w7 did strictly MORE work than
s77 and still finished inside the static run's 7,857 s total, while s77 alone took 16,770 s. Same
work, twice the time => environmental. Independent measurement of the effect size: the identical
fixA_clean binary on identical input took 13.9 s user under load and 5.06 s quieter, a 2.7x swing.
Cause: /usr/libexec/duetexpertd pinned at 99.9% CPU for 4h+ across the whole pool run and none of the
static run, MediaAnalysis at 209.8% afterwards, plus my own k=1 benchmarks run on the same box.
Re-run both on a quiet machine before drawing any conclusion about pooling.
